test_hosted_grant_inference_survives_owner_restart picked a free port, closed it,
then booted a daemon on it. Under CI's 16 parallel files another daemon-backed
test can bind that port first. The loser parks api_server
(api_server_port_in_use) but still reports ready. The winner, built from the
same fixtures (API key `ordinary-daemon-owned-secret`, echoing model stub),
answers every request. The invitation, grant and run all succeed against it, so
the hidden room session lands in the other daemon's state.db. This home's
lookup then returns None: the CI failure `assert None == ('bot_room', 1)`.

Repro: with a sibling daemon already listening on the port the test picks, the
old test fails with exactly that assertion and the same session id
(room_2c43d87a…). With this change it passes, because the boot detects the lost
port and re-picks.

Fix: a new `api_daemon()` fixture in local_recovery_probe. It boots, waits on
this daemon's own gateway_state.json (keyed on identify's pid) until api_server
settles, and re-boots on a fresh port when the state is `fatal`. The test
writes its config per boot and talks to the port the daemon actually owns.
